  "correction": "Lowered from the 2026-10-08 first recording: the eight readings cells (readings-r0-to-r3 and r2-covers-r1 across four papers) were recorded unmeasured on a loader defect in this directory, not on a corpus gap. A baseline is the record of a debt, so it comes down when the debt turns out not to exist. Raised 2026-10-10 for the four margin-entries cells only: they were recorded {count: 0, denominator: 0, met: false} because the cell was declared unmeasured, and they now measure against content/editorial/required-margin-entries/<paper>.yaml. Recording the real figures is a TIGHTENING, not a budget increase: mass-energy's 8 of 8 is now met in the baseline, so a regression to 7 of 8 is refused, where against met:false it would have counted as an improvement and passed. The three papers that are not met keep their real denominators so their counts cannot fall either. Also 2026-10-10, the four tour-present cells, for the same reason and in the same direction: they were recorded 0 of 0 met:false while the cell was unmeasured, and mass-energy's tour is present and resolving, so recording 1 of 1 met is what makes its disappearance a refusal rather than an improvement. And the four journey-skeleton-parts cells, 2026-10-10, correcting a claim I made in 5a3defd5: that commit said no baseline change was needed because 7 of 9 is not met in any paper. The ratchet catches two shapes, not one \u2014 a met item going unmet AND `cell.count < was.count` even while still unmet \u2014 so at count 0 a fall from 7 of 9 to 5 of 9 would have passed, because 5 is not less than 0. Recording 7 of 9 protects the seven parts that exist.",
